{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JRRBGb7ASW51dEMtqcbpTg","type":"tool_result","content":"150\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#paired_diff_aggregate]]\n151\t\\begin{table}[t]\n152\t\\centering\n153\t\\caption{Sealed final evaluation, primary cases P1--P6 (0.4 synthetic / 0.6 real), mean $\\pm$ standard deviation over eight seeds. The interval score is at level 0.90 (lower is better); coverage should be near 0.90. Bonferroni-Wilson shares the naive point estimate. Baseline coverage and width are replayed from the evaluator's code (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).}\n154\t\\label{tab:aggregate}\n155\t\\small\n156\t% @evidence[[tables/aggregate.tex]]\n157\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.mean_interval_score]]\n158\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.naive_baseline_score]]\n159\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.bonferroni_baseline_score]]\n160\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.resample_baseline_score]]\n161\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.coverage]]\n162\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.naive_signed_bias]]\n163\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json]]\n164\t% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json]]\n165\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n166\t\\toprule\n167\tReporter & Interval score $\\downarrow$ & Coverage & Mean width & Signed bias \\\\\n168\t\\midrule\n169\tNaive Wilson & 0.2202$\\pm$0.0234 & 0.713$\\pm$0.048 & 0.109 & 0.0366$\\pm$0.0029 \\\\\n170\tBonferroni-Wilson & 0.1974$\\pm$0.0036 & 0.962$\\pm$0.012 & 0.190 & 0.0366$\\pm$0.0029 \\\\\n171\tResampling & 0.2461$\\pm$0.0251 & 0.748$\\pm$0.020 & 0.104 & $-$0.0209$\\pm$0.0030 \\\\\n172\t\\textbf{EB-IRT (ours)} & 0.1449$\\pm$0.0040 & 0.929$\\pm$0.008 & 0.129 & 0.0121$\\pm$0.0027 \\\\\n173\t\\bottomrule\n174\t\\end{tabular}\n175\t\\end{table}\n176\t\n177\tEB-IRT attains a mean interval score of $0.1449$ against $0.2202$ (naive), $0.1974$ (Bonferroni-Wilson) and $0.2461$ (resampling) (Table~\\ref{tab:aggregate}), with coverage $0.929$ and RMSE $0.0348$. Its score is lower than each baseline's in all eight seeds; paired seed-level differences are $0.075\\pm0.021$ (naive), $0.053\\pm0.004$ (Bonferroni) and $0.101\\pm0.023$ (resampling). Its aggregate bias, $0.0121\\pm0.0027$, removes about two thirds of the naive optimism. The evaluator's qualification outcome for this run is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. Gate diagnostics beyond that outcome are not available to us, and the outcome does not by itself establish validity beyond the studied worlds.\n178\t\n179\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n180\t\\centering\n181\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/per_case_interval_score.pdf}\n182\t\\caption{Mean interval score per case (lower is better) for four reporters; bars show mean over eight seeds, whiskers one standard deviation across seeds. (a) Synthetic worlds including the null N1. (b) Real outcomes including the permuted null N2. The resampling bar for P2 is clipped (value 0.59).}\n183\t\\label{fig:perCase}\n184\t\\end{figure}\n185\t\n186\t\\begin{table}[t]\n187\t\\centering\n188\t\\caption{Per-case interval score (mean over eight seeds; best per row in bold) and coverage of the naive and EB-IRT intervals. ``exh.'' exhaustive search, ``halv.'' successive halving. Seed standard deviations are in the Supplement.}\n189\t\\label{tab:cases}\n190\t\\small\n191\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n192\t% @evidence[[tables/per_case.tex]]\n193\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json]]\n194\t% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json]]\n195\t\\begin{tabular}{llrrcccccc}\n196\t\\toprule\n197\tCase & World, search & $N$ & $K$ & Naive & Bonf. & Resamp. & EB-IRT & Cov.\\ naive & Cov.\\ EB-IRT \\\\\n198\t\\midrule\n199\tP1 & synthetic, exh. & 150 & 32 & 0.264 & 0.224 & 0.164 & \\textbf{0.150} & 0.65 & 0.96 \\\\\n200\tP2 & synthetic, halv. & 150 & 32 & 0.300 & 0.225 & 0.592 & \\textbf{0.155} & 0.61 & 0.96 \\\\\n201\tP3 & real, exh. & 200 & 24 & 0.155 & 0.161 & \\textbf{0.109} & 0.116 & 0.81 & 0.94 \\\\\n202\tP4 & real, halv. & 200 & 24 & 0.161 & 0.165 & 0.258 & \\textbf{0.156} & 0.77 & 0.89 \\\\\n203\tP5 & real, exh. & 60 & 24 & 0.303 & 0.295 & \\textbf{0.174} & 0.192 & 0.71 & 0.98 \\\\\n204\tP6 & real, exh. & 600 & 24 & 0.096 & 0.096 & \\textbf{0.092} & 0.096 & 0.78 & 0.83 \\\\\n205\t\\midrule\n206\tN1 & synth.\\ null, exh. & 150 & 32 & 0.354 & 0.231 & 0.140 & \\textbf{0.128} & 0.47 & 0.97 \\\\\n207\tN2 & real null, exh. & 200 & 24 & 0.218 & 0.168 & \\textbf{0.102} & 0.111 & 0.62 & 0.89 \\\\\n208\tZ1 & all-zero, exh. & 100 & 8 & 0.026 & 0.059 & 0.026 & \\textbf{0.020} & 1.00 & 1.00 \\\\\n209\t\\bottomrule\n210\t\\end{tabular}\n211\t\\end{table}\n212\t\n213\t\\subsection{Where the gain is large, and where it is not}\n214\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#by_searcher]]\n215\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases]]\n216\t% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json#cases]]\n217\tFigure~\\ref{fig:perCase} and Table~\\ref{tab:cases} show a heterogeneous picture, which the aggregate hides.\n218\t\n219\t\\emph{Large gains.} On synthetic worlds and at small $N$ EB-IRT lowers the naive interval score by 37--48\\% (P1: $0.150$ vs $0.264$; P2: $0.155$ vs $0.300$; P5: $0.192$ vs $0.303$) with coverage at or above nominal. It also beats Bonferroni-Wilson by $0.07$--$0.10$ in P1, P2 and P5 in every seed.\n220\t\n221\t\\emph{No gain over naive.} In real-outcome halving (P4) the three Wilson-style scores are close ($0.156$ vs naive $0.161$, Bonferroni $0.165$); the paired advantage over naive is $0.005$ and EB-IRT wins in six of eight seeds. Its point estimate is \\emph{more} biased than the naive one there ($0.031$ vs $0.025$) and its coverage is $0.89$. At $N=600$ (P6) all Wilson-style reporters score $0.096$ and the naive bias is already small ($0.009$ vs $0.011$ for EB-IRT).\n222\t\n223\t\\emph{Resampling is as good under exhaustive search.} Restricting the aggregate to exhaustive-search cases (P1 and P3, P5, P6, same weights) gives interval scores of $0.1406$ for EB-IRT and $0.1405$ for resampling, against $0.2166$ for naive: a tie. In the real exhaustive cases P3, P5 and N2 resampling is slightly better ($0.109$, $0.174$, $0.102$ vs $0.116$, $0.192$, $0.111$), and EB-IRT is the better of the two in only 2, 1 and 1 of eight seeds respectively. The aggregate advantage over resampling therefore comes from halving: on halving cases resampling scores $0.3916$ against $0.1556$, because it over-corrects the sparse log (bias $-0.067$ in P2 and $-0.031$ in P4; coverage $0.44$ and $0.61$). EB-IRT is the more robust reporter across searchers, not uniformly the more accurate.\n224\t\n225\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n226\t\\centering\n227\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/bias_and_coverage.pdf}\n228\t\\caption{Diagnostics for the primary cases (mean over eight seeds, whiskers one standard deviation across seeds). (a) Signed bias of the point estimate for the naive and EB-IRT reporters. (b) Empirical coverage of the 90\\% intervals; dashed line marks the nominal level. Naive coverage is replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n229\t\\label{fig:biascov}\n230\t\\end{figure}\n231\t\n232\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n233\t\\centering\n234\t\\includegraphics[width=0.7\\textwidth]{figures/width_vs_n.pdf}\n235\t\\caption{Mean interval width on the real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) as the number of benchmark questions grows (mean over eight seeds). EB-IRT is wider than the naive interval by a factor of 1.1--1.3 at each $N$ and much narrower than Bonferroni-Wilson. Naive and Bonferroni widths are replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n236\t\\label{fig:width}\n237\t\\end{figure}\n238\t\n239\t\\emph{Residual bias and miscalibrated width.} EB-IRT's signed bias is positive in five of six primary cases (range $0.009$--$0.031$) and slightly negative only in P1 ($-0.002\\pm0.010$) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a); shrinkage removes most, not all, of the winner's curse. Coverage varies from $0.98$ (P5) and $0.96$ (P1, P2) down to $0.89$ (P4) and $0.83$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}b). Interval width shrinks with $N$ for all reporters (Figure~\\ref{fig:width}): EB-IRT is $0.187$, $0.101$ and $0.065$ wide at $N=60$, $200$, $600$, against $0.167$, $0.088$ and $0.050$ for the naive interval and $0.284$, $0.153$ and $0.088$ for Bonferroni-Wilson. The aggregate coverage of $0.929$ is thus an average of over- and under-coverage rather than evidence of calibration in each regime: the fixed width factor over-widens the synthetic and small-$N$ cases and under-widens the largest-$N$ real case. At $N=600$ the target itself is measured on the remaining 900 pool questions, a finite-sample noise that no reporter here models and that matters most where intervals are narrowest; we did not test how much of the P6 shortfall it explains.\n240\t\n241\t\\subsection{Negative controls}\\label{sec:controls}\n242\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases.N1]]\n243\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases.N2]]\n244\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases.Z1]]\n245\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.null_control_abs_bias]]\n246\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.constant_control_error]]\n247\tIn the synthetic null N1 EB-IRT has coverage $0.971$ and interval score $0.128$ against $0.354$ for naive, whose coverage is $0.47$. In the permuted real null N2 EB-IRT has coverage $0.887$ and interval score $0.111$ (naive $0.218$, resampling $0.102$). In both nulls its bias is $0.017$: shrinkage does not reduce the estimate fully to the common mean, and the controls average $0.0173$ in absolute bias. In the constant-zero world Z1 the reporter returns exactly zero with interval $[0,0.02]$ (absolute estimate error $0$, interval score $0.020$ against $0.026$ naive and $0.059$ Bonferroni). The reporter therefore does not manufacture signal in null worlds, but its null coverage in the real world sits at the same $0.89$ as in P4.\n248\t\n249\t\\subsection{Reporter variants considered during development}\n250\t% @evidence[[analysis/search_variants.json]]\n251\tTwo alternative designs were evaluated in the development setting (four seeds, cases P1--P4 with controls) alongside EB-IRT: a parametric-bootstrap pivot calibration of the interval, and an extension with configuration-by-subject random effects. Table~\\ref{tab:variants} shows that narrower intervals do not pay: the bootstrap variant has the smallest width ($0.085$) but coverage $0.77$ and a higher interval score ($0.1562$) than EB-IRT ($0.1480$), and the interaction effects did not improve it ($0.1519$, coverage $0.893$). Each was evaluated once, so these differences are not a significance ranking; these runs are kept separate from the sealed evaluation, in which only EB-IRT was evaluated.\n252\t\n253\t\\begin{table}[t]\n254\t\\centering\n255\t\\caption{Development-setting comparison of reporter variants (four seeds; P1--P4 primary, N1--N2 for null-control coverage); single evaluation each, not a significance ranking. Baseline rows are the evaluator's reporters in the same setting.}\n256\t\\label{tab:variants}\n257\t\\small\n258\t% @evidence[[tables/search_variants.tex]]\n259\t% @evidence[[analysis/search_variants.json]]\n260\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n261\t\\toprule\n262\tVariant & Interval score & Coverage & Width & Bias & Null-control cov. \\\\\n263\t\\midrule\n264\tEB-IRT (frozen selection) & 0.1480 & 0.915 & 0.124 & 0.0143 & 0.933 \\\\\n265\t+ config$\\times$subject effects & 0.1519 & 0.893 & 0.120 & 0.0152 & 0.908 \\\\\n266\tBootstrap pivot calibration & 0.1562 & 0.770 & 0.085 & 0.0092 & 0.738 \\\\\n267\t\\midrule\n268\tNaive Wilson & 0.2321 & -- & -- & 0.0366 & -- \\\\\n269\tBonferroni-Wilson & 0.1961 & -- & -- & -- & -- \\\\\n270\tResampling & 0.2732 & -- & -- & -- & -- \\\\\n271\t\\bottomrule\n272\t\\end{tabular}\n273\t\\end{table}\n274\t\n275\t\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n276\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#aggregate_primary]]\n277\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#paired_diff_aggregate]]\n278\t% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json#aggregate_primary]]\n279\t\\textbf{What the evidence supports.} In the studied worlds, best-of-search reporting is optimistic by $0.037$ absolute on average and its nominal interval is badly calibrated, a gap that depends on the question budget and on how distinguishable the configurations are. A hierarchical posterior interval fitted to the log lowers the interval score by about a third relative to the naive report and by a quarter relative to Bonferroni-Wilson with lower width. These statements hold for eight seeds of a sealed run that completed with a qualified outcome.\n280\t\n281\t\\textbf{How to test a reporter (Q2).} Our results suggest what a reliability test should contain. (a) Worlds with known truth, so that coverage and bias are measured rather than inferred. (b) Interval score together with coverage, since either alone is gamed by extreme widths: Bonferroni-Wilson reaches coverage $0.96$ only by being $47\\%$ wider than EB-IRT. (c) Results by regime (search procedure, question budget, world), because the aggregate coverage of $0.929$ conceals cases from $0.83$ to $0.98$. (d) Negative controls: a null world to catch invented signal, a constant world to catch degenerate behaviour. (e) Seed-level uncertainty and paired differences between reporters, since the naive and resampling scores vary across seeds with standard deviations of $0.023$ and $0.025$, against $0.004$ for EB-IRT. (f) Baselines matched to the searcher: against resampling alone, the exhaustive-search verdict (a tie) differs from the halving verdict (a large gain).\n282\t\n283\t\\textbf{What it does not support.} (1) \\emph{Generality}: one 0.5B model whose accuracies (0.11--0.17) are close to the 10-option chance level of 0.10, one benchmark, one prompt family; the configuration spread and the optimism may differ for stronger models. (2) \\emph{Searchers}: only evaluator-owned exhaustive and halving searchers; no DSPy or MIPRO run, and no adaptive reporting of several scores in the manner of the Ladder. (3) \\emph{Decisions}: we measured the gap in a single configuration's accuracy, not whether it reverses rankings or deployment decisions; coverage and miss penalties show that reported numbers are unreliable, not how often a conclusion changes. (4) \\emph{Attribution}: the $\\kappa$ widening is hand-set and was never switched off, and subject means, EB variances and the Laplace correction were not ablated, so we cannot say how much coverage comes from the model versus the widening. The bias comparison is unaffected by $\\kappa$. (5) \\emph{Misspecification}: one-dimensional difficulty is a poor fit for real subject-by-configuration structure; the extension that added such structure did not help in development. (6) \\emph{Residual failures}: undercoverage at $N=600$ ($0.83$) and in real-outcome halving ($0.89$), positive bias in null worlds, and no gain over naive in P4 and P6. (7) \\emph{Theory}: ignorability is an argument, not a guarantee for misspecified models, and we offer no coverage theorem. (8) \\emph{Provenance}: the real outcome tensors were produced by the task authors and not regenerated, per-case diagnostics are labelled unverified, and the evaluator's gate diagnostics beyond the outcome are unavailable, so we make no claim about why individual checks passed. The qualified outcome does not turn these limits into guarantees.\n284\t\n285\t\\section{Conclusion}\n286\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.mean_interval_score]]\n287\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.naive_signed_bias]]\n288\t% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json#aggregate_primary.naive_cov]]\n289\tAfter automatic prompt search, a score reported on the search sample is an optimistic and over-confident description of fresh-question accuracy: across the studied worlds the naive best-of-search estimate is too high by about 0.04 on average, and its nominal 90\\% interval contains the truth about 71\\% of the time. A searcher-agnostic empirical-Bayes latent-difficulty posterior interval, computed from the search log alone, cut the interval score from 0.220 to 0.145 in a sealed eight-seed evaluation whose qualification outcome is ``qualified'', and reduced the bias by about two thirds. The improvement concentrates in synthetic worlds, at small question budgets, and under halving relative to resampling; for real exhaustive search a question-resampling correction is equally good, and for real halving and large question budgets the new reporter is no better than the naive interval and under-covers. We therefore recommend the reporter as a conditional, searcher-agnostic default for small samples, together with the evaluation protocol: known truth, null and constant controls, coverage next to interval score, and seed-level uncertainty. Open questions are whether the optimism alters rankings, how much of the coverage is owed to the width calibration, and whether the pattern holds for stronger models and real prompt optimizers.\n290\t\n291\t% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n292\t% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n293\t\\clearpage\n294\t\\bibliography{references}\n295\t\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n296\t\n297\t\\clearpage\n298\t\\appendix\n299\t\\section{Supplementary Information}\n300\t\\subsection{Estimator details and hyperparameters}\n301\tThe reporter is a single Python module with NumPy only. Fixed constants: at most 30 EB sweeps (three damped Newton steps for $a$ and for $b$ per sweep, steps clipped to $\\pm1$) with a 1.5\\,s wall-clock guard; $\\tau^2\\in[10^{-4},4]$, $s^2\\in[10^{-2},25]$; initial $\\tau^2=0.25$, $s^2=1$; five pseudo-questions for subject means; 400 posterior draws; 21 Gauss--Hermite nodes; level-shift draws with standard deviation $s/\\sqrt N$ and $\\log s$ perturbations with standard deviation $1/\\sqrt{2N}$. The prior variance of $a_{\\hat k}$ is floored at $0.3$ times the median inverse Schur precision so that near-equal configurations still receive an interval reflecting sampling noise. The world is treated as ``real'' when any subject code is nonzero. Table~\\ref{tab:kappa} gives the resulting $\\kappa$. The reporter's draws are seeded from the evaluator's reset call. Source: the reporter's module, function \\texttt{\\_kappa} and \\texttt{\\_fit\\_irt}.\n302\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/reporter_eb_irt/reporter.py]]\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[h]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\caption{Width factor $\\kappa$ applied around the estimate in each evaluation case (computed from the reporter's own function). It is not used in Z1, where the all-zero rule applies.}\n307\t\\label{tab:kappa}\n308\t\\small\n309\t% @evidence[[analysis/kappa_values.json]]\n310\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n311\t\\toprule\n312\tCase & $N$ & $K$ & Searcher & $\\kappa$ \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\tP1 & 150 & 32 & exhaustive & 1.435 \\\\\n315\tP2 & 150 & 32 & halving & 1.435 \\\\\n316\tP3 & 200 & 24 & exhaustive & 1.495 \\\\\n317\tP4 & 200 & 24 & halving & 1.495 \\\\\n318\tP5 & 60 & 24 & exhaustive & 1.632 \\\\\n319\tP6 & 600 & 24 & exhaustive & 1.425 \\\\\n320\tN1 & 150 & 32 & exhaustive & 1.435 \\\\\n321\tN2 & 200 & 24 & exhaustive & 1.495 \\\\\n322\t\\bottomrule\n323\t\\end{tabular}\n324\t\\end{table}\n325\t\n326\t\\subsection{Evaluation protocol and provenance}\n327\tThe sealed evaluation runs seeds 9101--9108; each seed runs P1--P6, N1, N2 (30 replications each) and Z1 (10), so $8\\times250=2000$ reporter calls. The development setting runs seeds 101--104 with cases P1--P4, N1, N2, Z1. Real worlds: MMLU-Pro revision \\texttt{b189ec76\\ldots} (MIT licence), Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct revision \\texttt{7ae55760\\ldots} (Apache-2.0), float32 CPU, one deterministic forward pass, prediction by argmax over the ten option-letter logits. Questions are deduplicated by normalized content hash, restricted to 10 options and at most 400 tokens, stratified over 14 subjects; the final pool has 1500 questions and the development pool 599, with no hash overlap between pools or with the demonstration source. The 24 configurations are indexed as (wording $\\times$ 3 + demonstration setting) $\\times$ 2 + format, with demonstration settings 0-shot and two 3-shot sets from the validation split. Configuration labels are randomly permuted per replication. Aggregate weights are 0.4 synthetic and 0.6 real. The interval score uses $\\alpha=0.10$ and a worst case of 20.\n328\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/final_manifest.json]]\n329\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#CASES]]\n330\t\n331\t\\textbf{Compute.} Evaluator-recorded wall-clock time per seed (250 episodes, covering reporter calls and scoring) averaged $6.2$\\,s, about $50$\\,s over the eight sealed seeds. No GPU or API was used in this study.\n332\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#episode_duration_seconds]]\n333\t\n334\t\\subsection{Per-case detail}\n335\tTable~\\ref{tab:suppcase} gives seed-level spread for all nine cases and Table~\\ref{tab:suppbase} the Bonferroni and resampling coverage and width.\n336\t\\begin{table}[h]\n337\t\\centering\n338\t\\caption{Per-case interval score (mean $\\pm$ s.d.\\ over eight seeds), width and signed bias for EB-IRT and the naive reporter. Naive width is replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n339\t\\label{tab:suppcase}\n340\t\\footnotesize\n341\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n342\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json]]\n343\t% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json]]\n344\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n345\t\\toprule\n346\tCase & IS EB-IRT & IS naive & Width EB-IRT & Width naive & Bias EB-IRT & Bias naive \\\\\n347\t\\midrule\n348\tP1 & 0.150$\\pm$0.009 & 0.264$\\pm$0.074 & 0.140 & 0.125 & $-$0.002$\\pm$0.010 & 0.049$\\pm$0.010 \\\\\n349\tP2 & 0.155$\\pm$0.006 & 0.300$\\pm$0.033 & 0.150 & 0.125 & 0.013$\\pm$0.005 & 0.054$\\pm$0.005 \\\\\n350\tP3 & 0.116$\\pm$0.015 & 0.155$\\pm$0.050 & 0.101 & 0.088 & 0.009$\\pm$0.007 & 0.022$\\pm$0.006 \\\\\n351\tP4 & 0.156$\\pm$0.027 & 0.161$\\pm$0.032 & 0.121 & 0.088 & 0.031$\\pm$0.004 & 0.025$\\pm$0.004 \\\\\n352\tP5 & 0.192$\\pm$0.012 & 0.303$\\pm$0.059 & 0.187 & 0.167 & 0.016$\\pm$0.008 & 0.051$\\pm$0.010 \\\\\n353\tP6 & 0.096$\\pm$0.014 & 0.096$\\pm$0.011 & 0.065 & 0.050 & 0.011$\\pm$0.003 & 0.009$\\pm$0.003 \\\\\n354\tN1 & 0.128$\\pm$0.004 & 0.354$\\pm$0.068 & 0.125 & 0.127 & 0.017$\\pm$0.004 & 0.067$\\pm$0.005 \\\\\n355\tN2 & 0.111$\\pm$0.018 & 0.218$\\pm$0.035 & 0.089 & 0.087 & 0.017$\\pm$0.003 & 0.034$\\pm$0.002 \\\\\n356\tZ1 & 0.020$\\pm$0.000 & 0.026$\\pm$0.000 & 0.020 & 0.026 & 0.000$\\pm$0.000 & 0.000$\\pm$0.000 \\\\\n357\t\\bottomrule\n358\t\\end{tabular}\n359\t\\end{table}\n360\t\\begin{table}[h]\n361\t\\centering\n362\t\\caption{Bonferroni-Wilson and resampling reporters per case: interval score (mean $\\pm$ s.d.\\ over eight seeds), and replayed coverage and width.}\n363\t\\label{tab:suppbase}\n364\t\\footnotesize\n365\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n366\t% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json]]\n367\t% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json]]\n368\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n369\t\\toprule\n370\tCase & IS Bonf. & IS Resamp. & Cov.\\ Bonf. & Cov.\\ Resamp. & Width Bonf. & Width Resamp. \\\\\n371\t\\midrule\n372\tP1 & 0.224$\\pm$0.004 & 0.164$\\pm$0.020 & 0.98 & 0.90 & 0.221 & 0.127 \\\\\n373\tP2 & 0.225$\\pm$0.006 & 0.592$\\pm$0.131 & 0.98 & 0.44 & 0.221 & 0.127 \\\\\n374\tP3 & 0.161$\\pm$0.009 & 0.109$\\pm$0.023 & 0.95 & 0.86 & 0.153 & 0.082 \\\\\n375\tP4 & 0.165$\\pm$0.016 & 0.258$\\pm$0.058 & 0.95 & 0.61 & 0.153 & 0.075 \\\\\n376\tP5 & 0.295$\\pm$0.021 & 0.174$\\pm$0.031 & 0.96 & 0.93 & 0.284 & 0.147 \\\\\n377\tP6 & 0.096$\\pm$0.008 & 0.092$\\pm$0.014 & 0.95 & 0.79 & 0.088 & 0.049 \\\\\n378\tN1 & 0.231$\\pm$0.007 & 0.140$\\pm$0.014 & 0.97 & 0.95 & 0.224 & 0.127 \\\\\n379\tN2 & 0.168$\\pm$0.013 & 0.102$\\pm$0.012 & 0.93 & 0.90 & 0.151 & 0.079 \\\\\n380\tZ1 & 0.059$\\pm$0.000 & 0.026$\\pm$0.000 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 0.059 & 0.026 \\\\\n381\t\\bottomrule\n382\t\\end{tabular}\n383\t\\end{table}\n384\t\n385\t\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n386\tAll numbers come from the sealed final record and the derived files in \\texttt{analysis/}. \\texttt{final\\_per\\_case.py} derives per-case means, seed standard deviations and paired differences from the recorded episodes; \\texttt{reconstruct\\_baselines.py} replays the evaluator's worlds and baselines (requires NumPy) and checks them against the recorded values; \\texttt{search\\_variants.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:variants}; \\texttt{kappa\\_values.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:kappa}; \\texttt{make\\_tables.py} writes Tables~\\ref{tab:aggregate}, \\ref{tab:cases}, \\ref{tab:variants}, \\ref{tab:kappa}, \\ref{tab:suppcase} and \\ref{tab:suppbase}; \\texttt{make\\_figures.py} writes Figures~\\ref{fig:perCase} and \\ref{fig:biascov}. The environment used Python 3.12, NumPy 2.5.4 and Matplotlib 3.11.2. Seeds are those listed above; running the scripts in the order given takes under a minute on CPU.\n387\t\n388\t\\subsection{LLM Usage}\n389\tLarge language models were used as research and writing assistants in this study: to propose and implement the reporter variants, to analyse the evaluation records, and to draft and edit this manuscript. All numerical results come from the evaluator's records and the scripts listed above; the authors of record are responsible for the content.\n390\t\n391\t\\end{document}\n392\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c0708d5a-e69d-4f2e-bad6-f6956008da35","uuid":"a5209186-8270-46bb-9e35-b7be61a06a17","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:58:55.447Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper.tex","content":"% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#paired_diff_aggregate]]\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final evaluation, primary cases P1--P6 (0.4 synthetic / 0.6 real), mean $\\pm$ standard deviation over eight seeds. The interval score is at level 0.90 (lower is better); coverage should be near 0.90. Bonferroni-Wilson shares the naive point estimate. Baseline coverage and width are replayed from the evaluator's code (Section~\\ref{sec:protocol}).}\n\\label{tab:aggregate}\n\\small\n% @evidence[[tables/aggregate.tex]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.mean_interval_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.naive_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.bonferroni_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.resample_baseline_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.coverage]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.naive_signed_bias]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json]]\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nReporter & Interval score $\\downarrow$ & Coverage & Mean width & Signed bias \\\\\n\\midrule\nNaive Wilson & 0.2202$\\pm$0.0234 & 0.713$\\pm$0.048 & 0.109 & 0.0366$\\pm$0.0029 \\\\\nBonferroni-Wilson & 0.1974$\\pm$0.0036 & 0.962$\\pm$0.012 & 0.190 & 0.0366$\\pm$0.0029 \\\\\nResampling & 0.2461$\\pm$0.0251 & 0.748$\\pm$0.020 & 0.104 & $-$0.0209$\\pm$0.0030 \\\\\n\\textbf{EB-IRT (ours)} & 0.1449$\\pm$0.0040 & 0.929$\\pm$0.008 & 0.129 & 0.0121$\\pm$0.0027 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nEB-IRT attains a mean interval score of $0.1449$ against $0.2202$ (naive), $0.1974$ (Bonferroni-Wilson) and $0.2461$ (resampling) (Table~\\ref{tab:aggregate}), with coverage $0.929$ and RMSE $0.0348$. Its score is lower than each baseline's in all eight seeds; paired seed-level differences are $0.075\\pm0.021$ (naive), $0.053\\pm0.004$ (Bonferroni) and $0.101\\pm0.023$ (resampling). Its aggregate bias, $0.0121\\pm0.0027$, removes about two thirds of the naive optimism. The evaluator's qualification outcome for this run is ``qualified'' with status ``completed''. Gate diagnostics beyond that outcome are not available to us, and the outcome does not by itself establish validity beyond the studied worlds.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/per_case_interval_score.pdf}\n\\caption{Mean interval score per case (lower is better) for four reporters; bars show mean over eight seeds, whiskers one standard deviation across seeds. (a) Synthetic worlds including the null N1. (b) Real outcomes including the permuted null N2. The resampling bar for P2 is clipped (value 0.59).}\n\\label{fig:perCase}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Per-case interval score (mean over eight seeds; best per row in bold) and coverage of the naive and EB-IRT intervals. ``exh.'' exhaustive search, ``halv.'' successive halving. Seed standard deviations are in the Supplement.}\n\\label{tab:cases}\n\\small\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n% @evidence[[tables/per_case.tex]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json]]\n\\begin{tabular}{llrrcccccc}\n\\toprule\nCase & World, search & $N$ & $K$ & Naive & Bonf. & Resamp. & EB-IRT & Cov.\\ naive & Cov.\\ EB-IRT \\\\\n\\midrule\nP1 & synthetic, exh. & 150 & 32 & 0.264 & 0.224 & 0.164 & \\textbf{0.150} & 0.65 & 0.96 \\\\\nP2 & synthetic, halv. & 150 & 32 & 0.300 & 0.225 & 0.592 & \\textbf{0.155} & 0.61 & 0.96 \\\\\nP3 & real, exh. & 200 & 24 & 0.155 & 0.161 & \\textbf{0.109} & 0.116 & 0.81 & 0.94 \\\\\nP4 & real, halv. & 200 & 24 & 0.161 & 0.165 & 0.258 & \\textbf{0.156} & 0.77 & 0.89 \\\\\nP5 & real, exh. & 60 & 24 & 0.303 & 0.295 & \\textbf{0.174} & 0.192 & 0.71 & 0.98 \\\\\nP6 & real, exh. & 600 & 24 & 0.096 & 0.096 & \\textbf{0.092} & 0.096 & 0.78 & 0.83 \\\\\n\\midrule\nN1 & synth.\\ null, exh. & 150 & 32 & 0.354 & 0.231 & 0.140 & \\textbf{0.128} & 0.47 & 0.97 \\\\\nN2 & real null, exh. & 200 & 24 & 0.218 & 0.168 & \\textbf{0.102} & 0.111 & 0.62 & 0.89 \\\\\nZ1 & all-zero, exh. & 100 & 8 & 0.026 & 0.059 & 0.026 & \\textbf{0.020} & 1.00 & 1.00 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Where the gain is large, and where it is not}\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#by_searcher]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json#cases]]\nFigure~\\ref{fig:perCase} and Table~\\ref{tab:cases} show a heterogeneous picture, which the aggregate hides.\n\n\\emph{Large gains.} On synthetic worlds and at small $N$ EB-IRT lowers the naive interval score by 37--48\\% (P1: $0.150$ vs $0.264$; P2: $0.155$ vs $0.300$; P5: $0.192$ vs $0.303$) with coverage at or above nominal. It also beats Bonferroni-Wilson by $0.07$--$0.10$ in P1, P2 and P5 in every seed.\n\n\\emph{No gain over naive.} In real-outcome halving (P4) the three Wilson-style scores are close ($0.156$ vs naive $0.161$, Bonferroni $0.165$); the paired advantage over naive is $0.005$ and EB-IRT wins in six of eight seeds. Its point estimate is \\emph{more} biased than the naive one there ($0.031$ vs $0.025$) and its coverage is $0.89$. At $N=600$ (P6) all Wilson-style reporters score $0.096$ and the naive bias is already small ($0.009$ vs $0.011$ for EB-IRT).\n\n\\emph{Resampling is as good under exhaustive search.} Restricting the aggregate to exhaustive-search cases (P1 and P3, P5, P6, same weights) gives interval scores of $0.1406$ for EB-IRT and $0.1405$ for resampling, against $0.2166$ for naive: a tie. In the real exhaustive cases P3, P5 and N2 resampling is slightly better ($0.109$, $0.174$, $0.102$ vs $0.116$, $0.192$, $0.111$), and EB-IRT is the better of the two in only 2, 1 and 1 of eight seeds respectively. The aggregate advantage over resampling therefore comes from halving: on halving cases resampling scores $0.3916$ against $0.1556$, because it over-corrects the sparse log (bias $-0.067$ in P2 and $-0.031$ in P4; coverage $0.44$ and $0.61$). EB-IRT is the more robust reporter across searchers, not uniformly the more accurate.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/bias_and_coverage.pdf}\n\\caption{Diagnostics for the primary cases (mean over eight seeds, whiskers one standard deviation across seeds). (a) Signed bias of the point estimate for the naive and EB-IRT reporters. (b) Empirical coverage of the 90\\% intervals; dashed line marks the nominal level. Naive coverage is replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n\\label{fig:biascov}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.7\\textwidth]{figures/width_vs_n.pdf}\n\\caption{Mean interval width on the real exhaustive cases ($K=24$) as the number of benchmark questions grows (mean over eight seeds). EB-IRT is wider than the naive interval by a factor of 1.1--1.3 at each $N$ and much narrower than Bonferroni-Wilson. Naive and Bonferroni widths are replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n\\label{fig:width}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\emph{Residual bias and miscalibrated width.} EB-IRT's signed bias is positive in five of six primary cases (range $0.009$--$0.031$) and slightly negative only in P1 ($-0.002\\pm0.010$) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}a); shrinkage removes most, not all, of the winner's curse. Coverage varies from $0.98$ (P5) and $0.96$ (P1, P2) down to $0.89$ (P4) and $0.83$ (P6) (Figure~\\ref{fig:biascov}b). Interval width shrinks with $N$ for all reporters (Figure~\\ref{fig:width}): EB-IRT is $0.187$, $0.101$ and $0.065$ wide at $N=60$, $200$, $600$, against $0.167$, $0.088$ and $0.050$ for the naive interval and $0.284$, $0.153$ and $0.088$ for Bonferroni-Wilson. The aggregate coverage of $0.929$ is thus an average of over- and under-coverage rather than evidence of calibration in each regime: the fixed width factor over-widens the synthetic and small-$N$ cases and under-widens the largest-$N$ real case. At $N=600$ the target itself is measured on the remaining 900 pool questions, a finite-sample noise that no reporter here models and that matters most where intervals are narrowest; we did not test how much of the P6 shortfall it explains.\n\n\\subsection{Negative controls}\\label{sec:controls}\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases.N1]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases.N2]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#cases.Z1]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.null_control_abs_bias]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.constant_control_error]]\nIn the synthetic null N1 EB-IRT has coverage $0.971$ and interval score $0.128$ against $0.354$ for naive, whose coverage is $0.47$. In the permuted real null N2 EB-IRT has coverage $0.887$ and interval score $0.111$ (naive $0.218$, resampling $0.102$). In both nulls its bias is $0.017$: shrinkage does not reduce the estimate fully to the common mean, and the controls average $0.0173$ in absolute bias. In the constant-zero world Z1 the reporter returns exactly zero with interval $[0,0.02]$ (absolute estimate error $0$, interval score $0.020$ against $0.026$ naive and $0.059$ Bonferroni). The reporter therefore does not manufacture signal in null worlds, but its null coverage in the real world sits at the same $0.89$ as in P4.\n\n\\subsection{Reporter variants considered during development}\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_variants.json]]\nTwo alternative designs were evaluated in the development setting (four seeds, cases P1--P4 with controls) alongside EB-IRT: a parametric-bootstrap pivot calibration of the interval, and an extension with configuration-by-subject random effects. Table~\\ref{tab:variants} shows that narrower intervals do not pay: the bootstrap variant has the smallest width ($0.085$) but coverage $0.77$ and a higher interval score ($0.1562$) than EB-IRT ($0.1480$), and the interaction effects did not improve it ($0.1519$, coverage $0.893$). Each was evaluated once, so these differences are not a significance ranking; these runs are kept separate from the sealed evaluation, in which only EB-IRT was evaluated.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-setting comparison of reporter variants (four seeds; P1--P4 primary, N1--N2 for null-control coverage); single evaluation each, not a significance ranking. Baseline rows are the evaluator's reporters in the same setting.}\n\\label{tab:variants}\n\\small\n% @evidence[[tables/search_variants.tex]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/search_variants.json]]\n\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n\\toprule\nVariant & Interval score & Coverage & Width & Bias & Null-control cov. \\\\\n\\midrule\nEB-IRT (frozen selection) & 0.1480 & 0.915 & 0.124 & 0.0143 & 0.933 \\\\\n+ config$\\times$subject effects & 0.1519 & 0.893 & 0.120 & 0.0152 & 0.908 \\\\\nBootstrap pivot calibration & 0.1562 & 0.770 & 0.085 & 0.0092 & 0.738 \\\\\n\\midrule\nNaive Wilson & 0.2321 & -- & -- & 0.0366 & -- \\\\\nBonferroni-Wilson & 0.1961 & -- & -- & -- & -- \\\\\nResampling & 0.2732 & -- & -- & -- & -- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#aggregate_primary]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#paired_diff_aggregate]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json#aggregate_primary]]\n\\textbf{What the evidence supports.} In the studied worlds, best-of-search reporting is optimistic by $0.037$ absolute on average and its nominal interval is badly calibrated, a gap that depends on the question budget and on how distinguishable the configurations are. A hierarchical posterior interval fitted to the log lowers the interval score by about a third relative to the naive report and by a quarter relative to Bonferroni-Wilson with lower width. These statements hold for eight seeds of a sealed run that completed with a qualified outcome.\n\n\\textbf{How to test a reporter (Q2).} Our results suggest what a reliability test should contain. (a) Worlds with known truth, so that coverage and bias are measured rather than inferred. (b) Interval score together with coverage, since either alone is gamed by extreme widths: Bonferroni-Wilson reaches coverage $0.96$ only by being $47\\%$ wider than EB-IRT. (c) Results by regime (search procedure, question budget, world), because the aggregate coverage of $0.929$ conceals cases from $0.83$ to $0.98$. (d) Negative controls: a null world to catch invented signal, a constant world to catch degenerate behaviour. (e) Seed-level uncertainty and paired differences between reporters, since the naive and resampling scores vary across seeds with standard deviations of $0.023$ and $0.025$, against $0.004$ for EB-IRT. (f) Baselines matched to the searcher: against resampling alone, the exhaustive-search verdict (a tie) differs from the halving verdict (a large gain).\n\n\\textbf{What it does not support.} (1) \\emph{Generality}: one 0.5B model whose accuracies (0.11--0.17) are close to the 10-option chance level of 0.10, one benchmark, one prompt family; the configuration spread and the optimism may differ for stronger models. (2) \\emph{Searchers}: only evaluator-owned exhaustive and halving searchers; no DSPy or MIPRO run, and no adaptive reporting of several scores in the manner of the Ladder. (3) \\emph{Decisions}: we measured the gap in a single configuration's accuracy, not whether it reverses rankings or deployment decisions; coverage and miss penalties show that reported numbers are unreliable, not how often a conclusion changes. (4) \\emph{Attribution}: the $\\kappa$ widening is hand-set and was never switched off, and subject means, EB variances and the Laplace correction were not ablated, so we cannot say how much coverage comes from the model versus the widening. The bias comparison is unaffected by $\\kappa$. (5) \\emph{Misspecification}: one-dimensional difficulty is a poor fit for real subject-by-configuration structure; the extension that added such structure did not help in development. (6) \\emph{Residual failures}: undercoverage at $N=600$ ($0.83$) and in real-outcome halving ($0.89$), positive bias in null worlds, and no gain over naive in P4 and P6. (7) \\emph{Theory}: ignorability is an argument, not a guarantee for misspecified models, and we offer no coverage theorem. (8) \\emph{Provenance}: the real outcome tensors were produced by the task authors and not regenerated, per-case diagnostics are labelled unverified, and the evaluator's gate diagnostics beyond the outcome are unavailable, so we make no claim about why individual checks passed. The qualified outcome does not turn these limits into guarantees.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.mean_interval_score]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.naive_signed_bias]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json#aggregate_primary.naive_cov]]\nAfter automatic prompt search, a score reported on the search sample is an optimistic and over-confident description of fresh-question accuracy: across the studied worlds the naive best-of-search estimate is too high by about 0.04 on average, and its nominal 90\\% interval contains the truth about 71\\% of the time. A searcher-agnostic empirical-Bayes latent-difficulty posterior interval, computed from the search log alone, cut the interval score from 0.220 to 0.145 in a sealed eight-seed evaluation whose qualification outcome is ``qualified'', and reduced the bias by about two thirds. The improvement concentrates in synthetic worlds, at small question budgets, and under halving relative to resampling; for real exhaustive search a question-resampling correction is equally good, and for real halving and large question budgets the new reporter is no better than the naive interval and under-covers. We therefore recommend the reporter as a conditional, searcher-agnostic default for small samples, together with the evaluation protocol: known truth, null and constant controls, coverage next to interval score, and seed-level uncertainty. Open questions are whether the optimism alters rankings, how much of the coverage is owed to the width calibration, and whether the pattern holds for stronger models and real prompt optimizers.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\n% body-page counting excludes reference-only pages.  Do NOT remove.\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\subsection{Estimator details and hyperparameters}\nThe reporter is a single Python module with NumPy only. Fixed constants: at most 30 EB sweeps (three damped Newton steps for $a$ and for $b$ per sweep, steps clipped to $\\pm1$) with a 1.5\\,s wall-clock guard; $\\tau^2\\in[10^{-4},4]$, $s^2\\in[10^{-2},25]$; initial $\\tau^2=0.25$, $s^2=1$; five pseudo-questions for subject means; 400 posterior draws; 21 Gauss--Hermite nodes; level-shift draws with standard deviation $s/\\sqrt N$ and $\\log s$ perturbations with standard deviation $1/\\sqrt{2N}$. The prior variance of $a_{\\hat k}$ is floored at $0.3$ times the median inverse Schur precision so that near-equal configurations still receive an interval reflecting sampling noise. The world is treated as ``real'' when any subject code is nonzero. Table~\\ref{tab:kappa} gives the resulting $\\kappa$. The reporter's draws are seeded from the evaluator's reset call. Source: the reporter's module, function \\texttt{\\_kappa} and \\texttt{\\_fit\\_irt}.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/reporter_eb_irt/reporter.py]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Width factor $\\kappa$ applied around the estimate in each evaluation case (computed from the reporter's own function). It is not used in Z1, where the all-zero rule applies.}\n\\label{tab:kappa}\n\\small\n% @evidence[[analysis/kappa_values.json]]\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n\\toprule\nCase & $N$ & $K$ & Searcher & $\\kappa$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nP1 & 150 & 32 & exhaustive & 1.435 \\\\\nP2 & 150 & 32 & halving & 1.435 \\\\\nP3 & 200 & 24 & exhaustive & 1.495 \\\\\nP4 & 200 & 24 & halving & 1.495 \\\\\nP5 & 60 & 24 & exhaustive & 1.632 \\\\\nP6 & 600 & 24 & exhaustive & 1.425 \\\\\nN1 & 150 & 32 & exhaustive & 1.435 \\\\\nN2 & 200 & 24 & exhaustive & 1.495 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Evaluation protocol and provenance}\nThe sealed evaluation runs seeds 9101--9108; each seed runs P1--P6, N1, N2 (30 replications each) and Z1 (10), so $8\\times250=2000$ reporter calls. The development setting runs seeds 101--104 with cases P1--P4, N1, N2, Z1. Real worlds: MMLU-Pro revision \\texttt{b189ec76\\ldots} (MIT licence), Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct revision \\texttt{7ae55760\\ldots} (Apache-2.0), float32 CPU, one deterministic forward pass, prediction by argmax over the ten option-letter logits. Questions are deduplicated by normalized content hash, restricted to 10 options and at most 400 tokens, stratified over 14 subjects; the final pool has 1500 questions and the development pool 599, with no hash overlap between pools or with the demonstration source. The 24 configurations are indexed as (wording $\\times$ 3 + demonstration setting) $\\times$ 2 + format, with demonstration settings 0-shot and two 3-shot sets from the validation split. Configuration labels are randomly permuted per replication. Aggregate weights are 0.4 synthetic and 0.6 real. The interval score uses $\\alpha=0.10$ and a worst case of 20.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/final_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#CASES]]\n\n\\textbf{Compute.} Evaluator-recorded wall-clock time per seed (250 episodes, covering reporter calls and scoring) averaged $6.2$\\,s, about $50$\\,s over the eight sealed seeds. No GPU or API was used in this study.\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#episode_duration_seconds]]\n\n\\subsection{Per-case detail}\nTable~\\ref{tab:suppcase} gives seed-level spread for all nine cases and Table~\\ref{tab:suppbase} the Bonferroni and resampling coverage and width.\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Per-case interval score (mean $\\pm$ s.d.\\ over eight seeds), width and signed bias for EB-IRT and the naive reporter. Naive width is replayed from the evaluator's code.}\n\\label{tab:suppcase}\n\\footnotesize\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json]]\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nCase & IS EB-IRT & IS naive & Width EB-IRT & Width naive & Bias EB-IRT & Bias naive \\\\\n\\midrule\nP1 & 0.150$\\pm$0.009 & 0.264$\\pm$0.074 & 0.140 & 0.125 & $-$0.002$\\pm$0.010 & 0.049$\\pm$0.010 \\\\\nP2 & 0.155$\\pm$0.006 & 0.300$\\pm$0.033 & 0.150 & 0.125 & 0.013$\\pm$0.005 & 0.054$\\pm$0.005 \\\\\nP3 & 0.116$\\pm$0.015 & 0.155$\\pm$0.050 & 0.101 & 0.088 & 0.009$\\pm$0.007 & 0.022$\\pm$0.006 \\\\\nP4 & 0.156$\\pm$0.027 & 0.161$\\pm$0.032 & 0.121 & 0.088 & 0.031$\\pm$0.004 & 0.025$\\pm$0.004 \\\\\nP5 & 0.192$\\pm$0.012 & 0.303$\\pm$0.059 & 0.187 & 0.167 & 0.016$\\pm$0.008 & 0.051$\\pm$0.010 \\\\\nP6 & 0.096$\\pm$0.014 & 0.096$\\pm$0.011 & 0.065 & 0.050 & 0.011$\\pm$0.003 & 0.009$\\pm$0.003 \\\\\nN1 & 0.128$\\pm$0.004 & 0.354$\\pm$0.068 & 0.125 & 0.127 & 0.017$\\pm$0.004 & 0.067$\\pm$0.005 \\\\\nN2 & 0.111$\\pm$0.018 & 0.218$\\pm$0.035 & 0.089 & 0.087 & 0.017$\\pm$0.003 & 0.034$\\pm$0.002 \\\\\nZ1 & 0.020$\\pm$0.000 & 0.026$\\pm$0.000 & 0.020 & 0.026 & 0.000$\\pm$0.000 & 0.000$\\pm$0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bonferroni-Wilson and resampling reporters per case: interval score (mean $\\pm$ s.d.\\ over eight seeds), and replayed coverage and width.}\n\\label{tab:suppbase}\n\\footnotesize\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json]]\n\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n\\toprule\nCase & IS Bonf. & IS Resamp. & Cov.\\ Bonf. & Cov.\\ Resamp. & Width Bonf. & Width Resamp. \\\\\n\\midrule\nP1 & 0.224$\\pm$0.004 & 0.164$\\pm$0.020 & 0.98 & 0.90 & 0.221 & 0.127 \\\\\nP2 & 0.225$\\pm$0.006 & 0.592$\\pm$0.131 & 0.98 & 0.44 & 0.221 & 0.127 \\\\\nP3 & 0.161$\\pm$0.009 & 0.109$\\pm$0.023 & 0.95 & 0.86 & 0.153 & 0.082 \\\\\nP4 & 0.165$\\pm$0.016 & 0.258$\\pm$0.058 & 0.95 & 0.61 & 0.153 & 0.075 \\\\\nP5 & 0.295$\\pm$0.021 & 0.174$\\pm$0.031 & 0.96 & 0.93 & 0.284 & 0.147 \\\\\nP6 & 0.096$\\pm$0.008 & 0.092$\\pm$0.014 & 0.95 & 0.79 & 0.088 & 0.049 \\\\\nN1 & 0.231$\\pm$0.007 & 0.140$\\pm$0.014 & 0.97 & 0.95 & 0.224 & 0.127 \\\\\nN2 & 0.168$\\pm$0.013 & 0.102$\\pm$0.012 & 0.93 & 0.90 & 0.151 & 0.079 \\\\\nZ1 & 0.059$\\pm$0.000 & 0.026$\\pm$0.000 & 1.00 & 1.00 & 0.059 & 0.026 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility}\nAll numbers come from the sealed final record and the derived files in \\texttt{analysis/}. \\texttt{final\\_per\\_case.py} derives per-case means, seed standard deviations and paired differences from the recorded episodes; \\texttt{reconstruct\\_baselines.py} replays the evaluator's worlds and baselines (requires NumPy) and checks them against the recorded values; \\texttt{search\\_variants.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:variants}; \\texttt{kappa\\_values.py} produces Table~\\ref{tab:kappa}; \\texttt{make\\_tables.py} writes Tables~\\ref{tab:aggregate}, \\ref{tab:cases}, \\ref{tab:variants}, \\ref{tab:kappa}, \\ref{tab:suppcase} and \\ref{tab:suppbase}; \\texttt{make\\_figures.py} writes Figures~\\ref{fig:perCase} and \\ref{fig:biascov}. The environment used Python 3.12, NumPy 2.5.4 and Matplotlib 3.11.2. Seeds are those listed above; running the scripts in the order given takes under a minute on CPU.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLarge language models were used as research and writing assistants in this study: to propose and implement the reporter variants, to analyse the evaluation records, and to draft and edit this manuscript. All numerical results come from the evaluator's records and the scripts listed above; the authors of record are responsible for the content.\n\n\\end{document}\n","numLines":243,"startLine":150,"totalLines":392}}}
